[s0l0!-header][h1]do...while Loops[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

A [b]do...while[/b] loop is similar to a [b]while [/b]loop, except that a [b]do...while[/b] loop is guaranteed to execute at least one time.
[s0l0!-header][b]Example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="java" codeId="742"]int x = 1;
[b]do [/b]{
  System.out.println(x);
  x++;
} [b]while[/b](x < 5);

/*
1
2
3
4
*/[/code]
Notice that the condition appears at the end of the loop, so the statements in the loop execute once before it is tested.
Even with a false condition, the code will run once. 
[s0l0!-header][b]Example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="java" codeId="743"]int x = 1;
[b]do [/b]{
  System.out.println(x);
  x++;
} [b]while[/b](x < 0);
[/code]
[note]Notice that in do…while loops, the while is just the condition and doesn't have a body itself.[/note]
